(GRM Discrepancy 3 - minimal repro. On 0.4.9 and earlier, append_stmt's
 second form was `"append" "each" name "from" expr "to" name
 ("treating" expr "as" expr)? "."` - treating placed AFTER "to name".
 That literal placement was a compile error ("Expected a statement, got
 Treating"). Placing `treating` where loop_expansion actually allowed
 it - right after "from expr", BEFORE "to name", matching print_stmt's
 placement (GRM-15) and loop_expansion's own production - parsed
 cleanly, but the substitution was a silent no-op: dst ended up
 [1, 2, 3], not [1, 20, 3], even though the identical treating clause DID
 substitute under `Print each` (GRM-16).
 RESOLVED, 2026-08-22 - vox #70, and the manual's grammar itself was
 corrected to match: LANGUAGE.md:5492-5493 now places `treating` right
 after `from expr`, matching how it always actually parsed. Re-run
 verbatim against 0.4.10: the substitution now fires.
 Ran: VOX_CORE_PATH=/home/josj/scr/english/vox/coreasm /home/josj/scr/english/vox/target/release/vox D3.vox -o p && ./p
 expected output:
   1
   20
   3
)
a list called src is [1, 2, 3].
a list called dst is [].
append each item from src treating 2 as 20 to dst.
Print each x from dst.
